# function hop(operation, value) { return operation(value); }
# const hops = [];
# for (let index = 0; index < 10; index++) hops.push(value => (value > 0 ? hop(hops[(value + index) % 10], value - 1) : value + index + 0.5));
# console.log(hop(hops[3], 1000000));
# -- end of source
# A million hops, each of which ends in a call of the next: no frame may stay behind. The calls of the ring are calls of values, so the ring returns boxed values (LinkedFunction::ringsOfTailCallsAreListed), and each call leaves by a jump, direct or not.
# `hop` is copied into the arrow function. There the operand has come out of a closed read, and nothing but Link's word about the parameter says that it is a closure of the one function: the copy takes that word and enters it directly.
# RUN: walk f2 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f2:Body
# The ring ends in calls of values, so what it gives passes no return that holds it to anything: the record of the call claims nothing of it (Plan::resultsPassNoReturn()).
# CHECK: [[result]]: {{*}} @JSValue = CallDirect {{*}} function=f2 {{*}} tail
# CHECK-NEXT: ReturnCall [[result]]
